---
/**
 * /calculator/sleep-debt — Sleep Debt Calculator Page
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Target keyword:    "sleep debt calculator"
 * Secondary:         "how much sleep debt do I have", "sleep deficit calculator", "sleep deprivation calculator"
 * Layout:            CalculatorLayout
 * Widget:            SleepDebtCalculator.astro
 */
import CalculatorLayout from '../../layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SleepDebtCalculator from '../../components/calculators/SleepDebtCalculator.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much sleep debt is too much?',
    answer: 'Any accumulated sleep debt above 5 hours per week is considered moderate and will begin causing noticeable impairments in cognitive performance, mood, and reaction speeds. An accumulated debt exceeding 10 hours per week is classified as severe sleep deprivation, creating significant metabolic, cardiovascular, and immunological health risks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you pay back sleep debt?',
    answer: 'Yes, sleep debt can be repaid, but it cannot be done in a single night. If you have accumulated 10 hours of sleep debt over a week, trying to sleep for 18 hours straight on Sunday will not fully restore your brain functions and will disrupt your circadian rhythm. Instead, debt must be paid back gradually by adding 30 to 60 minutes of sleep per night over a period of 1 to 2 weeks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does sleep debt affect muscle growth?',
    answer: 'Yes, sleep debt is highly detrimental to muscle growth (hypertrophy). Research demonstrates that sleep deprivation shifts your systemic hormone profile into a catabolic state, increasing muscle-wasting cortisol while decreasing testosterone and IGF-1. Additionally, sleep debt impairs muscle protein synthesis (MPS), meaning your body cannot repair muscle fibers damaged during workouts as effectively.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does it take to recover from sleep deprivation?',
    answer: 'For short-term sleep loss (such as one bad night), 1 to 2 nights of extended, high-quality sleep are typically sufficient to recover baseline cognitive function. For chronic sleep debt accumulated over months or years, it can take several weeks of consistent, optimal sleep hygiene to fully normalize metabolic, endocrine, and neurological baselines.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is napping a good way to repay sleep debt?',
    answer: 'Yes. Napping can be a highly effective tool to temporarily reduce sleep debt. A brief 20-to-30 minute power nap in the early afternoon (between 1 PM and 3 PM) can restore alertness, improve cognitive focus, and lower blood pressure without interfering with your ability to fall asleep at night. Avoid naps longer than 30 minutes, as you risk entering deep sleep and waking up with sleep inertia.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens to your body with chronic sleep debt?',
    answer: 'Chronic sleep debt causes widespread systemic downregulation. It compromises immune health (reducing white blood cell activity), decreases insulin sensitivity (raising type 2 diabetes risks), elevates resting blood pressure and arterial stiffness, increases visceral fat deposition, and accelerates cellular aging. Neurologically, it reduces grey matter activity and permanently slows down reaction times.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does sleep debt affect athletic performance?',
    answer: 'Sleep debt drastically degrades athletic performance. It reduces time-to-exhaustion during cardio sessions, lowers submaximal lifting capacity, decreases motor control and coordination, and impairs mental focus. Furthermore, studies show athletes with chronic sleep debt have a 1.7× higher risk of muscular and skeletal training injuries compared to those getting sufficient sleep.',
  },
];

const relatedCalculators = [
  {
    name: 'Sleep Cycles',
    href: '/calculator/sleep',
    emoji: '😴',
    description: 'Calculate ideal bedtimes and wake times',
  },
  {
    name: 'TDEE Calculator',
    href: '/calculator/tdee',
    emoji: '⚡',
    description: 'Find your total daily calorie burn',
  },
  {
    name: 'Heart Rate Zones',
    href: '/calculator/heart-rate',
    emoji: '❤️',
    description: 'Calculate target exercise zones',
  },
  {
    name: 'Water Intake',
    href: '/calculator/water',
    emoji: '💧',
    description: 'Find your baseline hydration targets',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  title="Sleep Debt Calculator — How Much Sleep Are You Missing?"
  description="Calculate your weekly sleep debt by entering your actual vs ideal sleep for each day. See your deficit in hours and get a personalized recovery plan. Free, private, instant."
  keyword="sleep debt calculator"
  canonicalPath="/calculator/sleep-debt"
  formula="Ideal vs Actual Weekly Sleep Cumulative Deficit Math"
  lastModified={new Date('2026-07-12')}
  faqs={faqs}
  relatedCalculators={relatedCalculators}
  accuracyRating="±5–10%"
>

  <!-- slot: calculator -->
  <SleepDebtCalculator slot="calculator" />

  <!-- slot: what-is -->
  <Fragment slot="what-is">
    <p>
      <strong>Sleep debt</strong> (also referred to as a sleep deficit) is the cumulative difference
      between the amount of sleep your body biologically requires and the amount of sleep you actually
      obtain. For example, if your body requires 8 hours of sleep per night to function optimally, but you
      only sleep for 6 hours, you have accumulated a 2-hour sleep debt for that night. Over a week, these
      missed hours compound, creating a systemic deficit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sleep science distinguishes between <strong>acute sleep debt</strong> (the deficit accumulated over a
      few days or a single week) and <strong>chronic sleep debt</strong> (which persists over months or years
      due to poor sleep schedules, insomnia, or work demands). While acute sleep debt is easily resolved with
      a few nights of recovery sleep, chronic sleep debt shifts your biological baselines, causing permanent
      adjustments to your metabolic rate, cardiovascular health, and cognitive performance.
    </p>
    <p>
      A common misconception is that sleep debt can be resolved by "sleeping in" on the weekend. While getting
      extra sleep on Saturday and Sunday can relieve temporary feelings of sleepiness, clinical studies show
      it does not reverse the insulin resistance, elevated blood pressure, and neural performance declines
      caused by a week of sleep deprivation. Sleep debt is a cumulative biological ledger that must be paid back
      gradually and systematically.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- slot: how-to -->
  <Fragment slot="how-to">
    <p>
      Sleep deprivation is not just a state of feeling tired; it is a severe physiological stressor that directly
      degrades physical fitness, athletic recovery, and body composition:
    </p>

    <h3>1. Endocrine Disruptions: Testosterone & Cortisol</h3>
    <p>
      A landmark clinical study by <strong>Leproult & Van Cauter (2011)</strong> demonstrated that restricting sleep
      to 5 hours per night for just one week reduced healthy young men\'s daytime <strong>testosterone levels by 10%
      to 15%</strong>. This is equivalent to aging their hormonal profile by nearly 10 to 15 years. Concurrently, sleep debt
      elevates systemic levels of <strong>cortisol</strong> (the primary stress hormone). Elevated cortisol promotes muscle
      tissue breakdown (catabolism) and increases abdominal fat storage.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Impaired Muscle Protein Synthesis (MPS)</h3>
    <p>
      Research published by <strong>Dattilo et al. (2011)</strong> analyzed the cellular pathways through which sleep
      deprivation causes muscle loss. They identified that sleep debt downregulates insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)
      and muscle protein synthesis (MPS) pathways while upregulating myostatin (a protein that limits muscle growth).
      Simply put, if you train hard but carry a high sleep debt, your body cannot physically synthesize the proteins
      required to build and repair muscle fibers.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Performance, Coordination & Injury Risk</h3>
    <p>
      Accumulating sleep debt degrades neural reaction times, visual tracking, and motor coordination to a degree
      comparable to alcohol intoxication. For lifters and athletes, this impairment significantly increases the risk of
      training injuries. A study on young athletes revealed that those sleeping fewer than 8 hours per night had a
      <strong>1.7 times higher risk of injury</strong> over a competitive season compared to those getting sufficient sleep.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- slot: ranges -->
  <Fragment slot="ranges">
    <p>
      Paying back accumulated sleep debt requires a systematic approach rather than crash sleeping:
    </p>

    <h3>How Sleep Debt Accumulates and Recovers</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Debt Level</th>
          <th>Deficit (Hours/Week)</th>
          <th>Physiological Symptoms</th>
          <th>Recommended Recovery Action</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Minimal</strong></td>
          <td>0 to 2 hours</td>
          <td>None; normal baseline health.</td>
          <td>Maintain current schedule consistency.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Mild</strong></td>
          <td>2 to 5 hours</td>
          <td>Slight daytime fatigue, mild focus loss.</td>
          <td>Add 30 min of sleep per night for 3–4 days.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Moderate</strong></td>
          <td>5 to 10 hours</td>
          <td>Irritability, reduced lifting power, slow reaction times.</td>
          <td>Add 60 min of sleep per night for 1–2 weeks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Severe</strong></td>
          <td>10+ hours</td>
          <td>Chronic fatigue, high cortisol, insulin resistance, immune compromise.</td>
          <td>Follow a structured 1-month recovery plan (+60 min/night).</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>The 10-Hour Rule: Why Weekend Binging Fails</h3>
    <p>
      If you finish the work week with a **12-hour sleep debt**, you cannot physically sleep for 20 hours on Saturday
      to erase the deficit. Doing so causes a circadian shift known as "social jetlag," making it impossible to fall
      asleep at a normal time on Sunday night, restarting the cycle of sleep deprivation. 
      The only scientifically validated recovery strategy is **progressive repayment**: add 45 to 60 minutes of extra
      sleep per night (or take short early afternoon naps) until the biological debt is fully resolved.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

</CalculatorLayout>
